"""Static prose for the v17 manuscript (parts whose wording does not depend on new numbers). Numbers that did not change keep their v16 wording."""

INTRO = [
"Support and resistance is one of the oldest ideas in technical analysis: a price at which a market has turned before is expected to stop or reverse it again, because traders remember it and leave orders there. Malaysian SnR is a recent version of the idea, spread mostly through YouTube, TikTok, TradingView scripts and online guides, and taught to retail traders of gold, silver, currencies and cryptocurrencies. It differs from the textbook version in three ways. Levels are single prices taken from candle closes, not zones drawn through wicks: an \"A\" level (resistance) forms where a bullish candle is followed by a bearish one, and a \"V\" level (support) where a bearish candle is followed by a bullish one. A level is \"fresh\" until price returns to it, so the method trades only the first retest. And a level that price closes through flips its role, so that broken resistance becomes support. Traders add a top-down process: a \"storyline\" on a higher timeframe sets the direction, \"roadblocks\" one timeframe lower are levels that might stop the move, and the trade is taken on a lower timeframe after a confirming candle. Teachers say the method finds where large traders have left orders, and some promotional material claims win rates of 70% or more.",
"MSNR is taught mainly for gold, silver, currencies and the large cryptocurrencies, which trade around the clock, are popular with young retail traders in South and Southeast Asia, and are cheap to start trading. Retail day traders as a group do badly; among the day traders of Taiwan studied by Barber et al. (2014), fewer than one in a hundred showed skill that persisted and covered their fees. A method that claims high win rates in these markets reaches many people who can little afford to lose, so testing it is worth doing whichever way the result goes.",
"When MSNR is written down as rules, do its entries do better than random entries with the same risk, and does anything survive costs? We test this on the markets where the method is taught, with the rule frozen before the new tests were run, and we report how much searching we did, so that a positive result could be judged against the number of tries. The method's own teachers say that judgement matters, so a mechanical test cannot show that no MSNR trader can profit. It can show whether the parts of the method that can be written down carry any information, how large that information is, and how large it would have to be to pay for trading it.",
"This paper extends an earlier version of the study (our earlier tests used about nine years of crypto data, three coins and 2.4 years of hourly futures data for metals). The rule and the random-entry design are unchanged. What is new is the evidence around them. First, the sample: 14 cryptocurrencies from Binance, including ten that the earlier version never tested and that serve as a confirmatory sample, and gold and silver spot bars from March and May 2009 to October 2026, about 17 years. Second, the benchmark: in addition to the original random-entry design, we match the direction mix of the strategy and its volatility state, and we add a benchmark of entries on rejection candles with no level at all. Third, inference: pooled results use a bootstrap that resamples calendar months, so that the strong correlation between coins does not overstate precision; every family of tests has Holm and false-discovery control; and we state the smallest edge the design could have detected. Fourth, the results are broken down by year, trend and volatility regime, and the cost needed to break even is reported. The earlier headline, that the entries were \"no better than random\", turns out to need a qualification, which we report in Section 3.",
]

RELATED = [
"Academic work gives the idea some support but not much. Osler (2000) found that support and resistance levels published by six firms predicted intraday trend interruptions in exchange rates better than arbitrary levels did, and Osler (2003) offered a reason: stop-loss and take-profit orders cluster at round numbers, so prices reverse at some levels and run through others. Kavajecz and Odders-White (2004) showed that support and resistance levels tend to sit where the limit order book holds the most depth, and Urquhart (2017) documented clustering of Bitcoin prices at round numbers. Chung and Bellotti (2021; arXiv preprint, https://arxiv.org/abs/2101.07410) and Garzarelli et al. (2014) found that prices bounce off detected levels more often than chance would suggest. Both also found that a level with more previous bounces is more likely to bounce again, which is the opposite of the \"fresh is best\" rule. Zapranis and Tsinaslanidis (2012) evaluated horizontal support and resistance levels on US stocks, and Henderson et al. (2025; arXiv preprint, https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.02331) gave a mathematical model in which a support-and-resistance line rule can be optimal, without testing it on data. None of these papers tests a rule that a trader could use after costs.",
"The trading-rule literature is more sceptical. Brock et al. (1992) reported that simple rules, including trading-range breaks, predicted returns on the Dow Jones index over almost a century, but Sullivan et al. (1999) showed that much of that result disappears once the best rule is judged against all 7,846 rules that could have been tried, and White (2000), Hansen (2005) and Romano and Wolf (2005) gave the formal tests. Lo et al. (2000) put pattern recognition on a statistical footing. Apparent winners do not persist and vanish after costs (Bajgrowicz and Scaillet 2012), a test of over 21,000 rules in 30 currencies that allows for data snooping still finds excess profits, but they vary over time and across regions (Hsu et al. 2016), and intraday filter rules from currency analysts did not make money on average (Curcio et al. 1997). Park and Irwin (2007) count positive results in 56 of 95 modern studies but find most of them open to data snooping and to doubts about risk and trading costs, and Menkhoff and Taylor (2007) describe foreign exchange professionals who keep using technical analysis. In cryptocurrencies, Corbet et al. (2019) found that moving-average and range-breakout rules looked significant on one-minute Bitcoin data before costs, and Hudson and Urquhart (2021) and Gerritsen et al. (2020) reported that simple rules did better on Bitcoin than on equity indices before costs, with much weaker results once trading costs and data snooping were taken into account. Wang et al. (2015) and Psaradellis et al. (2023) apply superior-predictive-ability and false-discovery methods to large rule sets in Chinese markets and in commodity spreads, and find that results depend on the sample period and the market.",
"Closer to this paper, several working papers from 2026 have tested methods taught on social media. Mahadzva (2026a; SSRN working paper, https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7430998) coded six \"smart money concepts\" rules, built on liquidity sweeps, order blocks, changes of character and fair value gaps, on intraday EURUSD, GBPUSD, AUDUSD and USDJPY. It found \"no evidence that ICT/SMC concepts work as literally taught\", and its best setup (p = 0.0192) failed the Deflated Sharpe test at every trial count. A series of papers by the same author applies the same protocol to moving-average, Bollinger and Donchian rules on four currency pairs, where none of 45 combinations passed, and to other retail ideas such as Fibonacci levels and session breakouts (Mahadzva 2026b, SSRN working paper, https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7430758). Darmanin (2026; arXiv preprint, https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.20093) tested five families of retail signals on US equity index series and stock panels against exposure-matched benchmarks; four families were refuted, trend was inconclusive and none was supported. Babayans (2026; SSRN working paper, https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7608245) coded a smart-money indicator as an intraday strategy on spot gold, using one-minute data from 2012 to 2026, and paired each signal with random entries that shared its side, stop and target, which is the design closest to ours. The timing component was indistinguishable from zero, and the returns that remained came from a slow trend filter and the exit rule. We read only the abstracts of the Mahadzva and Babayans papers. None of these papers tests Malaysian SnR, its fresh-level rule or its close-based levels. Searches of arXiv, Crossref, OpenAlex, MPRA and IDEAS/RePEc on 13 to 17 October 2026, in English, Malay and Indonesian, found no study of the method. SSRN, Google Scholar and Shodhganga could not be searched automatically, so we can only say that we found none.",
]

RULES = [
"We coded the method from the teaching sources: an MQL5 indicator by Vesath, open-source TradingView scripts (DoN_JCT, trading-guide, zacdivan, Jithuze), the Crow Investing guide, a SlideShare PDF, guides on Scribd and CourseHero, and a TikTok summary. We tagged each rule as consensus (taught by all the sources that address it), taught by some sources, or our own operationalisation, where no source gives a number. Online Resource 1 gives the full specification with these tags, and the Appendix lists the choices that are our own. Several sources could not be read in full (some YouTube pages, some Scribd documents, one TradingView script that returned an error), and some quoted wording came from search snippets.",
"In the simplest variant (V0, \"literal\"), a resistance level forms at the close of a bullish candle that is followed by a bearish one, and a support level at the close of a bearish candle followed by a bullish one. A level can be used from the bar after it forms. A new level is fresh. A bar retests a resistance if its high reaches the level, its close stays below, and the previous bar had not reached it; after a retest the level is unfresh until a candle body closes through it, which flips its role and makes it fresh again. The signal is a retest of a fresh level, short at resistance and long at support. The trade is entered at the next bar's open, with the stop just beyond the rejection wick (0.1 ATR beyond it) and the target at 2R, where R is the distance from entry to stop. We held one position at a time. V1 uses \"obvious\" levels instead, defined as closes that are higher (or lower) than the three closes on each side, and allows one role flip. V2 adds the storyline filter, which trades only in the direction of the last break of a higher-timeframe close pivot. V3 trades only Quasimodo levels, a three-pivot pattern that the sources treat as a reversal level. V4 takes its levels from the higher timeframe and its retests from the trading timeframe. Figure 1 shows the level, retest and flip rules on an illustrative price path.",
"The fuller top-down method (the canonical method of the earlier phase 3, which we do not re-run here; Section 3.10) follows the taught process across four timeframes. We tested three ladders: weekly, daily, 4-hour and 1-hour; daily, 4-hour, 1-hour and 15-minute; and 4-hour, 1-hour, 15-minute and 5-minute. A storyline starts when a fresh level on the top timeframe is retested and rejected, and it becomes active when, within two top-timeframe bars, a bar one timeframe down closes beyond its last swing in the same direction. It ends at the nearest fresh opposing level on the top timeframe, or when price closes back beyond the rejection wick. A point of interest is a touch of a fresh level on the third timeframe by a bar of the fourth. The trade is taken only in the storyline's direction, after an engulfing candle or a break of structure on the fourth timeframe, at the next bar's open, with the stop beyond the zone and a 3R target. It is skipped if a fresh opposing level on the second timeframe (a roadblock) lies between entry and target.",
"Some taught elements were left out or turned into proxies. We did not use gap levels, which most sources include, because 24-hour markets have almost no gaps between one close and the next open. Judgements such as whether a level \"has liquidity\" or is \"obvious\" became counted rules (a pivot depth, a count of nearby swing points, a count of near-misses), and these are not the same as a trader's eye. There is no entry refinement below 5 minutes, and no news avoidance or manual trade management.",
"The rule was frozen. The engine used for every new result in this paper is a byte-for-byte copy of the one used in the earlier version (SHA-256 543bb8c7...891e, archived with the code). We did not re-run the earlier eight-point parameter grid, and we did not test any other stop buffer, target or entry type on the new data. The five variants, the 0.1 ATR buffer and the 2R target are those that were fixed in code before any phase-1 result existed. Re-running the engine on the stored earlier data reproduced all 45 out-of-sample cells of the earlier phase 1 for Bitcoin, Ether and Solana: the trade counts were identical and gross and net R per trade agreed to within 0.00005.",
]

